1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:04,202
Imagine deleting a free game
to make room on your console.

2
00:00:04,495 --> 00:00:05,492
Nothing dramatic.

3
00:00:05,798 --> 00:00:07,008
You need the space.

4
00:00:07,314 --> 00:00:08,897
You'll download it again later.

5
00:00:09,202 --> 00:00:12,221
Except, when you go back,
the download is gone.

6
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:15,293
The game is still sitting on
somebody else's hard drive.

7
00:00:15,598 --> 00:00:16,889
Yours is just missing.

8
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:21,502
That happened with P.T., a horror game
built around a hallway you couldn't

9
00:00:21,522 --> 00:00:22,187
escape.

10
00:00:22,506 --> 00:00:24,953
Then its publisher
removed the way back in.

11
00:00:25,246 --> 00:00:29,594
A tiny advertisement became something
players were afraid to lose.

12
00:00:30,138 --> 00:00:34,766
And years after the game it advertised
was cancelled, someone moved the camera

13
00:00:34,846 --> 00:00:36,415
where you were never meant to look.

14
00:00:36,734 --> 00:00:38,410
There was still something behind you.

15
00:00:38,914 --> 00:00:43,801
To understand why people cared this much
about a free download, we need to go

16
00:00:43,821 --> 00:00:46,814
back to the night it was
pretending to be something else.

17
00:00:47,618 --> 00:00:49,874
August twelfth, twenty fourteen.

18
00:00:50,183 --> 00:00:54,577
Sony's Gamescom presentation
introduces a horror project called

19
00:00:54,597 --> 00:00:55,342
P.T.

20
00:00:55,639 --> 00:00:57,816
You can download it on PlayStation Four.

21
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:58,793
It's free.

22
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:03,064
The studio name is seven
seven eight zero S.

23
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,030
Which tells you approximately nothing.

24
00:01:05,562 --> 00:01:08,599
There isn't a familiar series
name doing the selling.

25
00:01:08,909 --> 00:01:12,870
You don't get to arrive with a shopping
list of things the sequel ought to do.

26
00:01:13,179 --> 00:01:14,159
You enter a house.

27
00:01:14,442 --> 00:01:17,596
There's a corridor, a corner, a few doors.

28
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:20,877
The sort of place you'd walk
through without remembering it.

29
00:01:21,394 --> 00:01:24,353
Walk to the end, go through
the door, and you're back.

30
00:01:24,662 --> 00:01:25,590
Same corridor.

31
00:01:25,899 --> 00:01:26,736
That's the trick.

32
00:01:27,058 --> 00:01:31,348
The game gives you so little space that
you start paying attention to every

33
00:01:31,368 --> 00:01:32,152
piece of it.

34
00:01:32,462 --> 00:01:33,129
A photo.

35
00:01:33,425 --> 00:01:34,027
A lamp.

36
00:01:34,324 --> 00:01:36,566
A doorway you really
don't want to inspect.

37
00:01:37,106 --> 00:01:37,825
P.T.

38
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:40,989
doesn't need to show you a
giant monster immediately.

39
00:01:41,286 --> 00:01:43,684
It makes you suspicious of the furniture.

40
00:01:43,994 --> 00:01:45,208
Very economical.

41
00:01:45,492 --> 00:01:47,096
Extremely unpleasant.

42
00:01:47,393 --> 00:01:51,888
And the more familiar the room becomes,
the less comfortable you are in it.

43
00:01:52,691 --> 00:01:55,787
In most games, knowing
the map makes you safer.

44
00:01:56,101 --> 00:01:57,091
You remember the route.

45
00:01:57,419 --> 00:01:59,292
You know where the enemies can come from.

46
00:01:59,634 --> 00:02:02,607
Here, familiarity gives the game a weapon.

47
00:02:02,922 --> 00:02:04,768
You know what the hallway
looked like last time.

48
00:02:05,069 --> 00:02:08,069
So a small change suddenly feels enormous.

49
00:02:08,609 --> 00:02:13,534
The official survival tips actually tell
players to inspect things and notice

50
00:02:13,554 --> 00:02:15,250
what's different between loops.

51
00:02:15,606 --> 00:02:18,484
Think about that instruction
inside a horror game.

52
00:02:18,812 --> 00:02:22,710
To progress, you have to stop
avoiding the strange detail.

53
00:02:23,038 --> 00:02:24,259
You have to go closer.

54
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:25,835
Congratulations.

55
00:02:26,149 --> 00:02:28,783
Curiosity is now a health hazard.

56
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:30,788
The corner does a lot of work.

57
00:02:31,103 --> 00:02:33,180
You can't see everything at once.

58
00:02:33,494 --> 00:02:34,796
The door does even more.

59
00:02:35,111 --> 00:02:39,385
A closed door is a promise that there
is something on the other side, and the

60
00:02:39,405 --> 00:02:42,677
game keeps making you decide
whether you're ready to find out.

61
00:02:43,238 --> 00:02:46,099
That's why merely describing
the layout makes P.T.

62
00:02:46,119 --> 00:02:48,087
sound smaller than it feels.

63
00:02:48,415 --> 00:02:51,891
The important space is the
bit you can't currently see.

64
00:02:52,192 --> 00:02:53,861
The corridor is limited.

65
00:02:54,176 --> 00:02:56,986
Your imagination has no budget limit.

66
00:02:57,301 --> 00:03:01,588
And then the game adds another problem:
you're not even sure what it wants you

67
00:03:01,608 --> 00:03:02,135
to do.

68
00:03:03,002 --> 00:03:03,352
P.T.

69
00:03:03,372 --> 00:03:07,778
makes observation feel useful, but it
doesn't hand you a friendly checklist.

70
00:03:08,079 --> 00:03:10,048
The audio contains clues.

71
00:03:10,336 --> 00:03:13,377
Some speech is reversed;
some isn't in English.

72
00:03:13,666 --> 00:03:18,173
You're dealing with a space that changes,
information you may not understand,

73
00:03:18,321 --> 00:03:21,260
and the possibility that you
missed a trigger three loops ago.

74
00:03:21,772 --> 00:03:24,634
So the experience spills
out of the console.

75
00:03:24,923 --> 00:03:26,216
People compare attempts.

76
00:03:26,517 --> 00:03:29,902
Someone gets further, somebody
else tries to reproduce it.

77
00:03:30,178 --> 00:03:32,402
A successful action becomes a theory.

78
00:03:32,703 --> 00:03:34,213
A theory becomes a ritual.

79
00:03:34,502 --> 00:03:39,469
And a ritual can spread faster than
anybody checks whether it was necessary.

80
00:03:40,028 --> 00:03:41,283
That last part matters.

81
00:03:41,584 --> 00:03:46,143
The community having a method doesn't
mean every step in the method causes

82
00:03:46,163 --> 00:03:46,755
something.

83
00:03:47,056 --> 00:03:51,386
When the rules are hidden, it's easy
to confuse the thing you did before

84
00:03:51,406 --> 00:03:54,013
progress with the thing
that produced progress.

85
00:03:54,314 --> 00:03:55,084
P.T.

86
00:03:55,181 --> 00:03:58,388
is a wonderful machine for
creating that uncertainty.

87
00:03:58,908 --> 00:04:03,773
Contemporary reporting says Kojima
expected the mystery to take at least a

88
00:04:03,793 --> 00:04:04,870
week to solve.

89
00:04:05,171 --> 00:04:07,281
Players broke through within hours.

90
00:04:07,543 --> 00:04:11,426
Imagine setting a puzzle for the internet
and expecting the internet to go to

91
00:04:11,446 --> 00:04:11,923
bed.

92
00:04:12,212 --> 00:04:14,730
The reward for finishing
wasn't just an exit.

93
00:04:15,030 --> 00:04:15,775
It was a name.

94
00:04:16,592 --> 00:04:19,785
At the end, a man walks
through a deserted street.

95
00:04:20,082 --> 00:04:22,142
His face is Norman Reedus.

96
00:04:22,452 --> 00:04:27,077
The credits connect the project to
Hideo Kojima and Guillermo del Toro.

97
00:04:27,386 --> 00:04:29,082
Then comes Silent Hills.

98
00:04:29,392 --> 00:04:33,838
Suddenly, the strange little download
is an announcement for something much

99
00:04:33,858 --> 00:04:34,499
bigger.

100
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:37,491
That reveal changes your
memory of the room.

101
00:04:37,775 --> 00:04:42,077
What looked like an unknown studio's
experiment is now a demonstration

102
00:04:42,097 --> 00:04:44,353
attached to people with reputations.

103
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:48,949
It's a sales pitch delivered after you've
already felt the thing it's selling.

104
00:04:49,246 --> 00:04:50,681
No trailer narrator needed.

105
00:04:50,991 --> 00:04:53,012
Your nervous system has seen enough.

106
00:04:53,536 --> 00:04:56,417
But there's a warning tucked
into this excitement.

107
00:04:56,713 --> 00:04:59,750
The teaser says it has no direct
relation to the main title.

108
00:05:00,034 --> 00:05:04,489
So we cannot treat the corridor as a
slice cut from a finished Silent Hills.

109
00:05:04,773 --> 00:05:08,513
We don't know that the larger game would
have kept this house, this structure,

110
00:05:08,849 --> 00:05:10,453
or this exact kind of fear.

111
00:05:10,976 --> 00:05:14,117
What players had was a
real, playable experience.

112
00:05:14,414 --> 00:05:16,630
What they were promised
was a future project.

113
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:19,742
Those two things are easy to
merge when you're excited.

114
00:05:20,052 --> 00:05:24,169
They become much harder to separate
when the future project disappears.

115
00:05:25,012 --> 00:05:29,268
By late April twenty fifteen,
the news had changed completely.

116
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:34,168
On April twenty seventh, Konami confirmed
that the Silent Hills project would

117
00:05:34,188 --> 00:05:35,345
not continue.

118
00:05:35,677 --> 00:05:38,643
The same statement
described it as embryonic.

119
00:05:38,949 --> 00:05:43,729
That's a pretty substantial detail when
this story gets retold as a finished

120
00:05:43,749 --> 00:05:45,930
masterpiece being thrown away.

121
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:48,296
We can mourn what might have been.

122
00:05:48,615 --> 00:05:51,181
We cannot review a game
that never arrived.

123
00:05:51,487 --> 00:05:55,942
There is no completed Silent Hills here
for us to compare against the version

124
00:05:56,075 --> 00:05:57,325
in everyone's head.

125
00:05:57,631 --> 00:06:01,115
The imaginary version gets
to be perfect forever.

126
00:06:01,434 --> 00:06:03,974
Actual games rarely get that luxury.

127
00:06:04,476 --> 00:06:09,417
And we don't need to invent a secret
conversation inside Konami to make

128
00:06:09,436 --> 00:06:10,806
the cancellation hurt.

129
00:06:11,099 --> 00:06:15,919
Kojima, del Toro, Reedus,
that hallway: the public

130
00:06:15,939 --> 00:06:19,543
had enough pieces to imagine
a remarkable collaboration.

131
00:06:19,849 --> 00:06:22,894
The announcement shut down
that particular future.

132
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:26,072
It did not cancel the
entire Silent Hill series.

133
00:06:26,596 --> 00:06:30,426
Normally, the teaser could
remain as an odd souvenir.

134
00:06:30,745 --> 00:06:34,123
A little demonstration from a
project that didn't work out.

135
00:06:34,429 --> 00:06:38,884
You could show a friend, explain the
context, and let them experience it.

136
00:06:39,189 --> 00:06:42,727
But a notice had already put a
deadline on that possibility.

137
00:06:43,557 --> 00:06:47,980
The notice said P.T.'s distribution
would end on April twenty ninth.

138
00:06:48,281 --> 00:06:49,401
That date matters.

139
00:06:49,715 --> 00:06:53,914
So does the next step, because these
are often squeezed into one event.

140
00:06:54,255 --> 00:06:56,533
First, the teaser left the store.

141
00:06:56,847 --> 00:07:01,169
Then, the normal route for previous
downloaders to get it again stopped

142
00:07:01,189 --> 00:07:02,046
working too.

143
00:07:02,568 --> 00:07:06,228
Reporting on May sixth described
the change in North America.

144
00:07:06,555 --> 00:07:10,259
For a short time after removal, people
who had already downloaded it could

145
00:07:10,279 --> 00:07:12,056
still return through their history.

146
00:07:12,371 --> 00:07:13,872
By that report, they couldn't.

147
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:15,482
Having obtained P.T.

148
00:07:15,502 --> 00:07:19,912
before was no longer the same as
having a fresh download available.

149
00:07:20,416 --> 00:07:23,497
That is a very different problem
from missing a promotion.

150
00:07:23,811 --> 00:07:26,391
If the game is installed,
you can play your copy.

151
00:07:26,706 --> 00:07:31,317
If you delete that installation, the
ordinary recovery path you expected may

152
00:07:31,337 --> 00:07:31,931
be gone.

153
00:07:32,232 --> 00:07:37,286
A routine storage decision has acquired
an unpleasant amount of consequence.

154
00:07:37,808 --> 00:07:42,481
To be precise: Konami did not
remotely wipe every installed copy.

155
00:07:42,782 --> 00:07:43,249
P.T.

156
00:07:43,269 --> 00:07:45,073
did not cease to exist.

157
00:07:45,374 --> 00:07:48,257
That distinction makes
the situation stranger.

158
00:07:48,558 --> 00:07:51,994
The experience survived,
but access was uneven.

159
00:07:52,295 --> 00:07:53,652
The hallway was still there.

160
00:07:53,966 --> 00:07:57,020
The door depended on which console
you were standing in front of.

161
00:07:57,859 --> 00:08:02,666
This is the point where an ordinary
console starts to feel like a container

162
00:08:02,791 --> 00:08:04,120
for something fragile.

163
00:08:04,435 --> 00:08:06,593
Not because the plastic has changed.

164
00:08:06,908 --> 00:08:08,672
Because of what's still on the drive.

165
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,776
A digital object, which once seemed
endlessly replaceable, suddenly

166
00:08:13,796 --> 00:08:16,375
has a very physical address.

167
00:08:16,890 --> 00:08:19,795
Notice how neatly that
reverses the original game.

168
00:08:20,110 --> 00:08:22,146
In the hallway, you wanted to leave.

169
00:08:22,474 --> 00:08:24,714
Outside it, people wanted a way back.

170
00:08:25,042 --> 00:08:29,007
The thing you couldn't escape became
the thing you might never get to enter.

171
00:08:29,308 --> 00:08:33,301
There is a pretty good horror story in
that, without adding a single ghost.

172
00:08:33,843 --> 00:08:38,184
A recording preserves a lot:
the lighting, the sounds, the

173
00:08:38,204 --> 00:08:39,656
surprise at the ending.

174
00:08:39,998 --> 00:08:42,373
But the video decides
when to turn the corner.

175
00:08:42,701 --> 00:08:44,126
Playing asks you to turn it.

176
00:08:44,454 --> 00:08:46,137
That hesitation belongs to you.

177
00:08:46,465 --> 00:08:51,313
A clip of somebody else being afraid
cannot completely preserve the decision

178
00:08:51,424 --> 00:08:52,645
that made them afraid.

179
00:08:53,203 --> 00:08:56,257
And keeping the software
matters for another reason.

180
00:08:56,572 --> 00:08:59,138
A recording shows what
somebody happened to see.

181
00:08:59,466 --> 00:09:03,988
The software may contain something that
nobody has looked at properly yet.

182
00:09:04,303 --> 00:09:07,168
In twenty nineteen, that
difference paid off.

183
00:09:07,989 --> 00:09:10,410
Lance McDonald investigated P.T.

184
00:09:10,595 --> 00:09:12,910
by changing how its camera behaved.

185
00:09:13,189 --> 00:09:17,677
In the sequence he demonstrated, after
the flashlight encounter, Lisa's

186
00:09:17,697 --> 00:09:20,436
character model follows behind the player.

187
00:09:20,742 --> 00:09:22,856
The camera normally moves with you.

188
00:09:23,162 --> 00:09:27,716
McDonald separated the viewpoint from
that movement and exposed what was

189
00:09:27,736 --> 00:09:29,027
happening out of sight.

190
00:09:29,533 --> 00:09:32,924
After that flashlight encounter,
the model follows you.

191
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:37,598
Turn around normally and your viewpoint
moves with the character, keeping the

192
00:09:37,618 --> 00:09:38,297
trick hidden.

193
00:09:38,616 --> 00:09:43,416
McDonald's detached camera lets us
see what an ordinary turn cannot.

194
00:09:43,962 --> 00:09:46,516
The distinction doesn't
make it less creepy.

195
00:09:46,808 --> 00:09:50,758
You're looking at a system designed
around your limited viewpoint.

196
00:09:51,010 --> 00:09:55,591
Some of the unsettling shadows
and sounds now have a visible

197
00:09:55,611 --> 00:09:56,476
explanation.

198
00:09:56,782 --> 00:10:01,708
The feeling that something is nearby
has an actual piece of game machinery

199
00:10:01,728 --> 00:10:02,487
behind it.

200
00:10:02,792 --> 00:10:05,040
Literally behind it, in this case.

201
00:10:05,645 --> 00:10:10,313
Four years after the cancellation,
the teaser still had a reveal left.

202
00:10:10,618 --> 00:10:14,116
A new one came from looking at
the old software differently.

203
00:10:14,435 --> 00:10:18,617
That is what gets lost when we reduce
preservation to keeping a video of the

204
00:10:18,637 --> 00:10:19,249
ending.

205
00:10:19,581 --> 00:10:22,095
Sometimes the interesting
part isn't in the ending.

206
00:10:22,414 --> 00:10:23,850
It's just outside the frame.

207
00:10:24,675 --> 00:10:26,966
Then the next console generation arrived.

208
00:10:27,267 --> 00:10:30,585
In twenty twenty, reviewers
reported that P.T.

209
00:10:30,649 --> 00:10:35,418
could initially be transferred to early
PlayStation Five review hardware and

210
00:10:35,438 --> 00:10:36,216
played there.

211
00:10:36,557 --> 00:10:39,835
For a moment, the hallway
appeared to have made the jump.

212
00:10:40,136 --> 00:10:41,874
Later, that stopped working.

213
00:10:42,416 --> 00:10:46,247
Sony told Polygon that blocking
it was a publisher decision.

214
00:10:46,561 --> 00:10:50,997
Buying a newer console hadn't opened a
permanent way back into the hallway.

215
00:10:51,528 --> 00:10:54,214
Think about the expectation
being interrupted.

216
00:10:54,502 --> 00:10:57,688
A new console is supposed to
move your library forward.

217
00:10:58,002 --> 00:11:02,351
In this case, the surviving installation
on the older machine remained

218
00:11:02,371 --> 00:11:03,069
important.

219
00:11:03,357 --> 00:11:07,772
Upgrading the hardware and preserving
this experience were no longer

220
00:11:07,792 --> 00:11:09,648
automatically the same thing.

221
00:11:10,179 --> 00:11:13,194
Those were tests on review
hardware in twenty twenty.

222
00:11:13,535 --> 00:11:17,247
Fan recreations and recovery
methods are another story.

223
00:11:17,561 --> 00:11:21,444
The ordinary option, sending a
friend the store link, was gone.

224
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:24,939
By now, there are really two P.T.

225
00:11:24,959 --> 00:11:25,811
stories.

226
00:11:26,129 --> 00:11:30,531
One is the thing people played:
a finite set of rooms, tricks,

227
00:11:30,651 --> 00:11:32,180
sounds, and puzzles.

228
00:11:32,486 --> 00:11:35,478
The other is the future
people built around it.

229
00:11:35,797 --> 00:11:40,245
Silent Hills, imagined from a
few names and a very effective

230
00:11:40,265 --> 00:11:41,076
nightmare.

231
00:11:41,606 --> 00:11:44,000
The second story can swallow the first.

232
00:11:44,319 --> 00:11:48,741
Every time somebody calls the cancelled
project the greatest horror game we

233
00:11:48,761 --> 00:11:52,431
never got, the imagined
version gets a little bigger.

234
00:11:52,723 --> 00:11:57,172
Meanwhile, the actual teaser is sitting
there doing something more interesting

235
00:11:57,192 --> 00:11:58,415
than being a promise.

236
00:11:58,734 --> 00:12:01,434
It works as an experience
you can discuss on its own.

237
00:12:01,988 --> 00:12:06,775
The corridor, the unreliable
familiarity, the need to look closer:

238
00:12:07,161 --> 00:12:08,929
those are concrete choices.

239
00:12:09,248 --> 00:12:12,054
You can argue about whether
the puzzles are satisfying.

240
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:16,802
You can find the atmosphere effective
and the final triggers frustrating.

241
00:12:17,121 --> 00:12:21,675
It doesn't have to be flawless to deserve
a future beyond somebody's memory of

242
00:12:21,695 --> 00:12:22,001
it.

243
00:12:22,510 --> 00:12:27,411
And the camera investigation gives us
a better reason to care than nostalgia

244
00:12:27,431 --> 00:12:28,056
alone.

245
00:12:28,375 --> 00:12:32,936
Preserving an interactive work keeps
the possibility of discovering

246
00:12:32,989 --> 00:12:34,146
something new in it.

247
00:12:34,452 --> 00:12:37,298
Even when the advertising
campaign is over.

248
00:12:37,603 --> 00:12:41,048
Even when the thing being
advertised is never coming out.

249
00:12:41,875 --> 00:12:42,208
P.T.

250
00:12:42,228 --> 00:12:44,169
began by hiding what it was.

251
00:12:44,497 --> 00:12:49,232
It ended up showing us how easily
access to a digital experience can

252
00:12:49,252 --> 00:12:50,704
become uncertain.

253
00:12:51,032 --> 00:12:53,544
The mystery inside the
house had an answer.

254
00:12:53,858 --> 00:12:57,942
The fate of the project outside it was
something players couldn't solve by

255
00:12:57,962 --> 00:13:00,174
staring at another photograph.

256
00:13:00,707 --> 00:13:02,946
That is why the deletion story sticks.

257
00:13:03,247 --> 00:13:05,596
Not because every copy was erased.

258
00:13:05,924 --> 00:13:09,604
Because a game could still exist
and still be out of reach.

259
00:13:09,905 --> 00:13:14,749
Its survival depended, in part, on
people keeping an old installation

260
00:13:14,769 --> 00:13:18,300
instead of trusting that another
download would always be waiting.

261
00:13:18,856 --> 00:13:21,979
The promise of Silent
Hills remains a what-if.

262
00:13:22,321 --> 00:13:27,247
The teaser is something people actually
made, actually played, and kept

263
00:13:27,267 --> 00:13:28,732
finding things inside.

264
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:33,959
It deserves to be remembered as more
than a trailer for an imaginary perfect

265
00:13:33,979 --> 00:13:34,438
game.

266
00:13:34,973 --> 00:13:39,138
So here's the question I'd leave in that
hallway: if you could preserve only

267
00:13:39,158 --> 00:13:44,111
one thing, would you keep the original
playable experience, or accept a

268
00:13:44,131 --> 00:13:48,082
perfect recording of everything
we currently know is in it?

269
00:13:48,397 --> 00:13:50,963
After Lisa, I'm keeping the controller.
